course: dictionary
lesson: 12149
title: Words that push and pull
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Compel means to force someone to do something. «Students are compelled to wear uniform» means the school rules make them do it, whether they want to or not.
explain: Compel can also mean to grab your attention so strongly that you cannot look away. «The film had a compelling plot» means the story is so interesting you want to keep watching. Compelling is the adjective: a compelling story, compelling evidence.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. A rule, a pressure or a strong feeling often tells you compel means force. «She felt compelled to help the lost puppy.» Swap in «felt she had to help»: if it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Compel means to force someone to do something.
fact: Compel is a formal word, often used for rules and laws.
fact: To be compelled to do something means you have to do it, or feel you must.
fact: «Compelling» means very interesting or convincing.
fact: To compel attention means to make people look at or listen to something closely.
exercise: «Students are compelled to wear uniform.» What does compelled mean here? | Allowed to, if they want | Kept warm | Asked nicely to | *Forced to, by a rule
exercise: «The detective found compelling evidence.» What does compelling mean? | Tired and slow | *Very convincing and interesting | Very small | Hidden under the bed
exercise: «She felt compelled to help the lost puppy.» What does this tell us? | She did not want to help at all | She forgot about the puppy | *She felt she had to help | She helped a cat instead
test: Which word means to force someone to do something? | compose | colour | *compel | comfort
test: «The story was so ___ that I could not stop reading.» Choose the best word. | *compelling | tiny | dull | sleepy
exam: «The school rules ___ every pupil to arrive on time.» Which word fits best? | collect | *compel | cancel | comfort
